course: dictionary
lesson: 6796
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Order is a good example. In «Put the names in alphabetical order», order means the way things follow one after another, such as A, B, C.
explain: Now look at «Mum placed an order for two pizzas.» Here order means something you ask to buy. The words around it, placed and pizzas, are your clues. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: In «The king's order was that the gates must close at dusk», an order is a command, something you must do. Same word, three different jobs. Read the words around it to find the right one.
fact: Order means the way things follow one after another, such as «in order of size».
fact: To order means to ask for something to be bought, as in «order a pizza». An order is also the thing you asked for.
fact: «In order» means tidy or working well, and «out of order» means broken.
fact: An order can be a command, so to order someone means to tell them what to do.
fact: Order is a noun and a verb: order, orders, ordered, ordering.
exercise: «Please write the names in alphabetical order.» What does order mean here? | Broken | *The way things follow one after another | A command | A meal you asked for
exercise: «The lift is out of order, so use the stairs.» What does out of order mean? | Very quick | Sold out | Tidy and working well | *Broken
exercise: Ben says, «I'll order a sandwich at the café.» What does order mean here? | Put things in a line | *Ask to buy something | Break something | Tell someone what to do
test: «The captain gave the order to set sail at dawn.» What does order mean here? | A meal you asked for | The way things follow one after another | *A command, telling someone what to do | Broken
test: Put 9, 3 and 6 in order from least to greatest. Which list is right? | 9, 6, 3 | *3, 6, 9 | 3, 9, 6 | 6, 9, 3
exam: Which phrase has the words in the right order, as in «Adjectives usually go in this order: opinion, size, age»? | box old lovely little | *a lovely little old box | little box lovely old | old little lovely box
